            {"1_ID": "P012", "2_Title": "Argon2: Memory-Hard Password Hashing", "3_Year": 2015, "4_Authors": "Biryukov, A.; Dinu, D.; Khovratovich, D.", "5_Venue": "Password Hashing Competition", "6_URL": "https://github.com/P-H-C/phc-winner-argon2", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "Memory-hard KDF GPU/ASIC resistant. 1GB memory, ~1 second CPU. GPU 10-100× slower. Linux crypt, MySQL, PostgreSQL, Ruby, PHP.", "9_Keywords": "password-hashing, memory-hard, GPU-resistant, ASIC-resistant, argon2id, key-derivation", "10_Threat_Model": "GPU/ASIC attacks, offline dictionary attacks, brute-force", "11_Security_Goals": "High memory+time cost, GPU resistance, ASIC resistance, parallelism scaling", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Memory access time uncorrelated, parallelism possible. DOES NOT HANDLE: Specialized ASIC", "13_Concept_1": "Memory Hardness: Fills large array (~1GB) during derivation. GPU memory-limited 10-100× slower. Linearly increases cost.", "14_Concept_2": "Time Cost: Iterations parameter. Higher → slower per-guess. Tuning ~1 second per guess (user-acceptable).", "15_Concept_3": "Parallelism: Lanes parameter allows parallel (SIMD). Tradeoff faster legitimate vs GPU harder.", "16_Concept_4": "Variants: Argon2i (time-memory tradeoff resistant), Argon2d (faster), Argon2id (hybrid, recommended).", "17_Concept_5": "Adoption: Linux crypt (glibc 2.36+), MySQL, PostgreSQL, Ruby, PHP. Migration PBKDF2 → Argon2.", "18_Proofs": "PHC winner evaluated GPU/ASIC. 10-100× GPU disadvantage. Empirical evaluation strong.", "19_Experiments": "CPU Intel Xeon, AMD EPYC. GPU NVIDIA Tesla, AMD Radeon. Derivation time, memory, GPU speedup.", "20_Implementation": "https://github.com/P-H-C/phc-winner-argon2 (C) | libsodium | Ruby argon2 | PHP password_hash"},
            {"1_ID": "P013", "2_Title": "Ring Confidential Transactions (RingCT)", "3_Year": 2016, "4_Authors": "Shen Noether; Mackenzie, A.", "5_Venue": "Monero Research Lab", "6_URL": "https://github.com/monero-project/research-lab", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "Privacy protocol hiding cryptocurrency amounts via ring signatures + commitments. Sender proves amount valid range. Ring signatures hide among decoys. Monero default since 2017. 1.7KB typical.", "9_Keywords": "privacy, ring-signatures, confidential-transactions, cryptocurrency, monero, amount-hiding", "10_Threat_Model": "Blockchain observer, transaction graph analysis, sender unlinkability, amount confidentiality", "11_Security_Goals": "Sender anonymity, amount confidentiality, zero-knowledge proofs, no double-spending", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Discrete-log hard, commitments binding. DOES NOT HANDLE: Timing analysis, IP leaks", "13_Concept_1": "Ring Signatures: Sender signs real key + decoys. 1 of n anonymity. Non-linkable prevents blackmail.", "14_Concept_2": "Pedersen Commitments: C = a*G + r*H (amount, randomness). Verifier C_in = C_out without learning amounts.", "15_Concept_3": "Range Proofs: Prove amount ∈ [0, 2^64]. Bulletproofs ~600B per output (logarithmic).", "16_Concept_4": "Linkability Prevention: Key images prevent reuse. Verifier checks unique per transaction.", "17_Concept_5": "Deployment: Monero mandatory Sept 2017. ~1.7KB per tx (vs Bitcoin 226B, overhead for privacy). 200GB chain.", "18_Proofs": "RingCT unlinkable + zero-knowledge amount proofs. Soundness: no double-spending.", "19_Experiments": "Monero mainnet 4.5M transactions. Transaction size, verification time, blockchain size.", "20_Implementation": "https://github.com/monero-project/monero (C++) | monero-cli | apt install monero | Docker oficialmonero/monero"},
            {"1_ID": "P014", "2_Title": "Zcash: Privacy-Preserving Cryptocurrency", "3_Year": 2014, "4_Authors": "Ben-Sasson, E.; Chiesa, A.", "5_Venue": "IEEE S&P 2014", "6_URL": "https://z.cash/", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "Blockchain zk-SNARK shielded transactions. Sender proves validity (inputs ≥ outputs, no double-spend) without revealing sender/receiver/amount. ~200B proof, ~10ms verification. 2016-present. ~$2B market cap.", "9_Keywords": "zero-knowledge, cryptocurrency, zk-SNARK, shielded-transactions, privacy, blockchain, Groth16", "10_Threat_Model": "Blockchain deanonymization, sender/receiver privacy, amount confidentiality", "11_Security_Goals": "Sender/receiver anonymity, amount confidentiality, zk-SNARK verification, no double-spend", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: zk-SNARK sound (trusted setup), discrete-log hard. DOES NOT HANDLE: Timing analysis, IP leaks", "13_Concept_1": "zk-SNARKs: Sender proves inputs + spending key without revealing. Groth16 pairing (~200B, ~10ms).", "14_Concept_2": "Shielded Pools: Public sum(inputs)=sum(outputs). Private sender/receiver via zk (no linkability).", "15_Concept_3": "Sprout (2016) vs Sapling (2018): Slow ~40s → fast ~5s. Smaller proofs. Orchard (2021) improvements.", "16_Concept_4": "Trusted Setup: Ceremony (2016) 6 participants. Transparent commitments. Any 1 honest → secure.", "17_Concept_5": "Adoption: Mainnet 2016-present. ~$2B market. 2-3% shielded tx (complexity, regulatory). Niche but important.", "18_Proofs": "Ben-Sasson et al. 2014: zk-SNARK soundness + Groth16 + random oracle.", "19_Experiments": "Mainnet 10M+ tx (2016-2024). Shielded %, proof generation ~5s Sapling, ~10ms verification.", "20_Implementation": "https://z.cash | https://github.com/zcash/zcash (C++) | zcashd | Docker zcash/zcashd | Ywallet"},
            {"1_ID": "P015", "2_Title": "PBKDF2: Password-Based Key Derivation", "3_Year": 2000, "4_Authors": "Kaliski, B.", "5_Venue": "RFC 2898", "6_URL": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2898", "7_DOI": "10.17487/RFC2898", "8_Abstract": "HMAC-based KDF iteration count. PBKDF2(password, salt, iterations, length). ≥100k iterations (NIST), ≥600k modern. ~100ms 100k iterations. TLS, WiFi WPA2, PGP.", "9_Keywords": "password-derivation, HMAC-based, iteration-count, salt, key-stretching, TLS, PBKDF2", "10_Threat_Model": "Dictionary attacks, offline brute-force, rainbow tables", "11_Security_Goals": "PRF derivation, iteration-based cost, salt prevents precomputation", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: HMAC-PRF secure, iteration adequate. DOES NOT HANDLE: GPU acceleration 10-100×", "13_Concept_1": "Iteration-Based: HMAC iteratively c times (default c weak, modern c=100k-600k). Cost = c × HMAC per guess.", "14_Concept_2": "Salt: 16-32B random per password. Prevents rainbow tables. Unique computation per salt.", "15_Concept_3": "Key Expansion: Output arbitrary length via HMAC chaining. U_1, U_2, ... concatenate.", "16_Concept_4": "Comparison: PBKDF2 (universal), scrypt (memory-hard), Argon2 (state-of-art, GPU-resistant).", "17_Concept_5": "Deployment: TLS key derivation, WiFi WPA2, PGP, OpenSSL. Recommendation Argon2 for new systems.", "18_Proofs": "Krawczyk 1997: HMAC-PRF secure. c iterations linear cost.", "19_Experiments": "OpenSSL benchmark. Iterations/second on CPU. Parameter tuning.", "20_Implementation": "RFC 2898 | OpenSSL enc -aes-256-cbc -iter 100000 | Python hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac | Rust pbkdf2"},
            {"1_ID": "P016", "2_Title": "scrypt: KDF with Time & Memory Costs", "3_Year": 2009, "4_Authors": "Percival, C.", "5_Venue": "USENIX Security 2009", "6_URL": "https://www.tarsnap.com/scrypt.html", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "Memory-hard KDF balancing security vs ASIC attacks. N=2^14 (16MB typical), r=8, p=1-4. ~100ms 16MB. GPU 10-100× slower (memory-bound). Bitcoin key derivation, cryptocurrency wallets.", "9_Keywords": "KDF, memory-hard, scrypt, ROMix, salsa20, ASIC-resistance, key-derivation", "10_Threat_Model": "ASIC/GPU attacks, memory bandwidth bottleneck, brute-force", "11_Security_Goals": "Memory hardness, ASIC resistance, CPU-friendly, time-memory tradeoff", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Memory bandwidth fixed (ASIC cannot parallelize). DOES NOT HANDLE: Specialized ASIC", "13_Concept_1": "ROMix: Fills large memory (Salsa20 keystream). Reads random location, writes. GPU parallelization hard.", "14_Concept_2": "Parameters: N memory (2^14 typical), r block size (8), p parallelism (1-4). Tuning N=2^20 (1GB) strong security.", "15_Concept_3": "Salsa20: Stream cipher for pseudorandom fill. 64B → 64B. Used deterministically inside ROMix.", "16_Concept_4": "Comparison: scrypt (memory-hard, moderate GPU resistance), Argon2 (newer, stronger GPU resistance).", "17_Concept_5": "Deployment: Bitcoin (BIP38), Litecoin (scrypt mining), wallets. ~100ms per derivation (tunable).", "18_Proofs": "Percival 2009: Time-memory tradeoff hardness. GPU 10-100× slower (memory-bound).", "19_Experiments": "CPU Intel, AMD. GPU NVIDIA, AMD. ASIC Ethereum miner. Memory, derivation time, GPU speedup.", "20_Implementation": "https://www.tarsnap.com/scrypt.html | OpenSSL enc -aes-256-cbc -K -S | Rust scrypt | Python scrypt"},
            {"1_ID": "P017", "2_Title": "bcrypt: Adaptive Blowfish Hashing", "3_Year": 1999, "4_Authors": "Provos, N.; Mazières, D.", "5_Venue": "USENIX 1999", "6_URL": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bcrypt", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "Adaptive password hashing Blowfish cipher. Cost factor 2^cost iterations. Cost=12 ~250ms. GPU 10-20× faster (not memory-hard). PHP password_hash default, Linux, Django.", "9_Keywords": "password-hashing, Blowfish, adaptive, cost-factor, salt-included, password-security", "10_Threat_Model": "Brute-force password attacks, cost parameter tunable", "11_Security_Goals": "Adaptive cost, salt-included, Blowfish non-linear, hardware-adaptive", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Blowfish non-linear, cost adequate. DOES NOT HANDLE: GPU acceleration 10-20×", "13_Concept_1": "Blowfish: 64-bit blocks, 56-byte keys. Rounds 16 (expensive). bcrypt iterates 2^cost times per hash.", "14_Concept_2": "Adaptive Cost: Encodes cost in hash string ($2b$12$...). Extract cost, apply iterations. Rehashing higher cost.", "15_Concept_3": "Salt Inclusion: 16B random mixed. Output 60B includes cost, salt, hash. Simplifies implementation.", "16_Concept_4": "Comparison: bcrypt (established, slow on GPU), Argon2 (modern, memory-hard). Migrate bcrypt → Argon2.", "17_Concept_5": "Deployment: PHP password_hash (5.5+), Linux /etc/shadow, Django, Ruby. 25 years trusted.", "18_Proofs": "Provos & Mazières 1999: Blowfish + cost. Empirical 25 years.", "19_Experiments": "CPU i7, Ryzen. GPU GTX, RX. Hash time vs cost, GPU speedup 10-20×.", "20_Implementation": "PHP password_hash | Python bcrypt | Rust bcrypt | Ruby bcrypt | Installation apt install php"},
            {"1_ID": "P018", "2_Title": "Off-The-Record (OTR) Messaging", "3_Year": 2004, "4_Authors": "Borisov, N.; Goldberg, I.; Ustaoglu, B.", "5_Venue": "USENIX Security 2004", "6_URL": "https://otr.im/", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "IM protocol deniable authentication + forward secrecy. AKE handshake. AES-CTR + MAC per message. Session keys deleted per ratchet. Deniability: anyone can forge (no signature).", "9_Keywords": "messaging, deniability, OTR, forward-secrecy, deniable-authentication, instant-messaging", "10_Threat_Model": "Network eavesdropper, passive compromise, active MITM (limited), deniable auth prevents non-repudiation", "11_Security_Goals": "IND-CPA, authentication (off-record), forward secrecy, deniability (no proof authorship)", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: DH secure, hash collision-resistant. DOES NOT HANDLE: Endpoint compromise, side-channels", "13_Concept_1": "AKE Handshake: Three-way DH key exchange. Both: generate DH, commit, reveal, verify. Shared secret, session keys.", "14_Concept_2": "Message Encryption: Per-message key derivation. AES-CTR, HMAC. Counter increments (prevents replay).", "15_Concept_3": "Deniable Authentication: MAC from shared secret (only participants know). Verifier authenticates, cannot prove third-party.", "16_Concept_4": "Forward Secrecy: Session keys deleted per ratchet (~message rotation). Even if DH compromised, past secret.", "17_Concept_5": "Adoption: Pidgin, Adium (macOS). Signal Protocol similar (improved security). OTR influenced modern messaging.", "18_Proofs": "Borisov et al. 2004: DH + hash authentication. Forward secrecy via deletion.", "19_Experiments": "Pidgin + OTR plugin. Handshake latency, message overhead.", "20_Implementation": "https://otr.im | Pidgin OTR | LibOTR (C) | Python pure-python-otr"},
            {"1_ID": "P019", "2_Title": "WPA3: Wireless Security Standard", "3_Year": 2018, "4_Authors": "Wi-Fi Alliance", "5_Venue": "Wi-Fi Alliance Specification", "6_URL": "https://www.wi-fi.org/", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "WiFi security SAE (Simultaneous Authentication of Equals) handshake. Password-authenticated DH (Dragonfly). Individual data encryption. 192-bit option. 2018+ gradual adoption.", "9_Keywords": "WiFi, WPA3, SAE, wireless-security, Dragonfly, password-authenticated, encryption", "10_Threat_Model": "WiFi eavesdropper, passive observer, weak password dictionary attacks, KRACK key reuse", "11_Security_Goals": "Password-authenticated key agreement, dictionary-attack resistance, multi-user encryption, forward secrecy", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Dragonfly sound (ECC), password entropy. DOES NOT HANDLE: Weak passwords, side-channels", "13_Concept_1": "SAE (Dragonfly): Password → curve point (hash-to-curve). DH exchange. Authentication via hash. Dictionary-attack resistant online.", "14_Concept_2": "PMKID: Pre-Shared Master Key ID (unique per network). 4-way handshake derives per-client traffic keys.", "15_Concept_3": "Opportunistic Wireless Encryption (OWE): Open network encryption. Each client DH derive own key.", "16_Concept_4": "Protected Management Frames (PMF): Integrity-protected beacons, deauths. Prevents DoS attacks. Mandatory WPA3.", "17_Concept_5": "Deployment: 2018 standardized, slow adoption (hardware cycles). Migration WPA2 → WPA3 gradual. ~30% (2025).", "18_Proofs": "Harkins et al. Dragonfly: Password-authenticated DH secure (requires online verification).", "19_Experiments": "WiFi 6 routers, iPhone 12+, Samsung S20+, Windows 11, macOS 11+. Handshake latency, throughput.", "20_Implementation": "Wi-Fi Alliance spec | OpenWrt hostapd | wpa_supplicant | iOS 13+, Android 10+, Windows 11, macOS 11+"},
            {"1_ID": "P020", "2_Title": "SSH Protocol: Secure Shell", "3_Year": 1996, "4_Authors": "Ylonen, T.", "5_Venue": "IETF Standards Track", "6_URL": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc4251", "7_DOI": "10.17487/RFC4251", "8_Abstract": "Secure remote login/command execution. SSH Transport (encryption, authentication), SSH User Auth (password, public-key). DH/ECDH key exchange. AES-CTR, ChaCha20-Poly1305 encryption. Ubiquitous Linux, Unix, macOS, Windows.", "9_Keywords": "SSH, remote-access, public-key, authentication, secure-shell, terminal, encryption", "10_Threat_Model": "Network eavesdropper, passive MITM (prevented by host key), active MITM (user-trusted fingerprints)", "11_Security_Goals": "IND-CPA encryption, server authentication, user authentication, integrity, no-replay", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: DH secure, host key trusted, public-key sound. DOES NOT HANDLE: Client malware, weak passwords", "13_Concept_1": "SSH Transport: Encrypted channel. DH key agreement, derives symmetric keys (encryption+integrity). Server authentication public key.", "14_Concept_2": "User Authentication: Public-key (recommended), password (deprecated), keyboard-interactive (TOTP). SSH Transport → User Auth layer.", "15_Concept_3": "Channel Multiplexing: Single TCP carries multiple channels (shell, sftp, tunneling). Independent encryption per channel.", "16_Concept_4": "Port Forwarding: ssh -L local:remote (tunnel). ssh -R (reverse). Access internal services securely.", "17_Concept_5": "Deployment: Universal Linux/Unix, macOS, Windows. OpenSSH (de facto), libssh, Paramiko (Python), Dropbear (embedded).", "18_Proofs": "RFC 4251: DH hardness + signature security. Design-based.", "19_Experiments": "OpenSSH Linux, macOS, Windows. Key exchange latency, encryption throughput, auth time.", "20_Implementation": "RFC 4251-4254 | OpenSSH: https://www.openssh.com (BSD) | libssh | apt install openssh-server openssh-client"},
            {"1_ID": "P021", "2_Title": "Kyber: ML-KEM Key Encapsulation", "3_Year": 2022, "4_Authors": "Bos, J.; Ducas, L.; et al.", "5_Venue": "NIST Post-Quantum Standardization", "6_URL": "https://pq-crystals.org/kyber/", "7_DOI": "https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/634", "8_Abstract": "Post-quantum KEM selected NIST (Aug 2022) as ML-KEM. Module-Learning-With-Errors (Module-LWE) lattice. Kyber768 192-bit security. ~200µs ARM, ~50µs x86. 1184B ciphertext. Hybrid TLS 1.3.", "9_Keywords": "post-quantum, lattice-KEM, Module-LWE, IND-CCA2, NIST-standard, ML-KEM, quantum-resistant", "10_Threat_Model": "Quantum-capable adversary (~2030+), passive eavesdropper, harvest-now-decrypt-later", "11_Security_Goals": "Post-quantum confidentiality (IND-CCA2), quantum-resistant key agreement, deterministic decryption", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Module-LWE hard, constant-time decryption. DOES NOT HANDLE: Very early quantum computers", "13_Concept_1": "Module-LWE: Given (A, As+e), recover s. A random matrix, s secret, e small error. Quantum-resistant (assumed).", "14_Concept_2": "Fujisaki-Okamoto: IND-CPA → IND-CCA2 (proven reduction). Hash message → deterministic decrypt → hash result.", "15_Concept_3": "Ring Arithmetic: Z_q[x]/(x^256+1), q=3329 (NTT-friendly). NTT fast multiply. 32-byte key, 1184-byte ciphertext.", "16_Concept_4": "Hardware: ~5µs x86 (AVX2), ~200µs ARM (NEON). Competitive RSA-2048 (~100µs traditional).", "17_Concept_5": "Deployment: NIST (FIPS 203, Dec 2024). OpenSSL 3.0+. Hybrid 2024-2028. PQC standard decades.", "18_Proofs": "Kyber768 IND-CCA2 under Module-LWE. ~128-bit post-quantum security.", "19_Experiments": "x86 (i7), ARM (A72). Encapsulation ~200µs ARM, ~50µs x86.", "20_Implementation": "https://github.com/pq-crystals/kyber (C) | liboqs | OpenSSL 3.0+ | Docker liboqs-c"},
            {"1_ID": "P022", "2_Title": "Dilithium: ML-DSA Signature Scheme", "3_Year": 2022, "4_Authors": "Ducas, L.; Kiltz, E.; et al.", "5_Venue": "NIST Post-Quantum Cryptography", "6_URL": "https://pq-crystals.org/dilithium/", "7_DOI": "https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/633", "8_Abstract": "Post-quantum signature selected NIST as ML-DSA. Module-LWE based. Dilithium3 192-bit security. ~5ms sign/verify. 2500B signatures. Deployed hybrid TLS 1.3 2024+.", "9_Keywords": "post-quantum, lattice-signature, Module-LWE, unforgeability, NIST-standard, ML-DSA, Dilithium", "10_Threat_Model": "Quantum-capable adversary (~2030+), signature forgery, existential forgery under chosen-message attack", "11_Security_Goals": "Post-quantum unforgeability (EUF-CMA), 192-bit security, deterministic signing", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Module-LWE hard, hash collision-resistant. DOES NOT HANDLE: Quantum Grover (2^96)", "13_Concept_1": "Module-LWE Signatures: Private key = low-norm secret s. Public key A*s = b (mod q). Sign: hash msg, compute witness y.", "14_Concept_2": "Rejection Sampling: Sign rejects if signature norms too high. Ensures EUF-CMA (no bias). Average 2.8 attempts.", "15_Concept_3": "Compression: Public key ~1300B, signature ~2500B. Private key ~3000B. Larger than classical (trade quantum security).", "16_Concept_4": "Hardware: ~5ms sign/verify ARM (Cortex-A72). Faster than classical RSA-2048 signatures (~10ms).", "17_Concept_5": "Deployment: NIST (FIPS 204, Dec 2024). Hybrid TLS 1.3 (2024+). Full PQC by 2030.", "18_Proofs": "EUF-CMA under Module-LWE + random oracle (Fiat-Shamir). 192-bit post-quantum.", "19_Experiments": "ARM (A72), x86. Sign ~5ms, verify ~3ms. Key/signature size benchmarks.", "20_Implementation": "https://github.com/pq-crystals/dilithium (C) | liboqs | OpenSSL 3.0+ | Docker liboqs-c"},
            {"1_ID": "P023", "2_Title": "SPHINCS+: Post-Quantum Hash-Based Signatures", "3_Year": 2019, "4_Authors": "Bernstein, D.J.; Hopwood, D.; et al.", "5_Venue": "NIST Post-Quantum", "6_URL": "https://sphincs.org/", "7_DOI": "https://eprint.iacr.org/2015/529", "8_Abstract": "Hash-based signature scheme SPHINCS+. Stateless, no quantum breaks via hash. 200B signatures SPHINCS+-128f. ~1ms sign. Slower than lattice but provably secure hash.", "9_Keywords": "post-quantum, hash-based, signatures, stateless, one-time-signatures, Merkle-tree, SPHINCS", "10_Threat_Model": "Quantum-capable adversary, signature forgery, no state required", "11_Security_Goals": "Post-quantum unforgeability, hash-only security, 128-bit/256-bit options", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Hash function collision-resistant, XOF secure. DOES NOT HANDLE: Quantum Grover (2^128)", "13_Concept_1": "Hash-Only Security: Security reduces to collision + XOF (no algebra required). Proven: hash collision → forged signature.", "14_Concept_2": "Merkle Tree: Uses FORS one-time sigs + Merkle tree layers. Signature = FORS + Merkle path (~200B).", "15_Concept_3": "Stateless: No state required (unlike SPHINCS). SPHINCS+ uses randomization per signature (stateless).", "16_Concept_4": "Performance: ~1ms sign, ~10ms verify. Slower than lattice but simple, proven security.", "17_Concept_5": "Adoption: Alternative NIST finalists. Less deployed than ML-DSA (slower, larger). Research interest.", "18_Proofs": "EUF-CMA under collision-resistant hash + XOF (random oracle model).", "19_Experiments": "Sign ~1ms, verify ~10ms. Signature 200B (SPHINCS+-128f). Memory ~1-2KB.", "20_Implementation": "https://sphincs.org | https://github.com/jedisct1/libsodium (includes SPHINCS+) | Installation apt install libsodium"},
            {"1_ID": "P024", "2_Title": "HKDF: HMAC-based Extract-and-Expand", "3_Year": 2010, "4_Authors": "Krawczyk, H.", "5_Venue": "RFC 5869", "6_URL": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc5869", "7_DOI": "10.17487/RFC5869", "8_Abstract": "Simple KDF based HMAC. Extract: compress input to PRF. Expand: stretch to output. TLS 1.3 standard. Two steps: PRF reduction + length extension.", "9_Keywords": "KDF, HMAC-based, extract-expand, PRF, TLS-1.3, key-derivation, HKDF", "10_Threat_Model": "Weak input entropy, insufficient output length, entropy leakage", "11_Security_Goals": "PRF property, entropy extraction, output independence", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: HMAC-PRF secure, sufficient input entropy. DOES NOT HANDLE: Non-independent inputs", "13_Concept_1": "Extract: HMAC(salt, input) → PRF (compresses entropy). Salt can be empty (zeros). Stretches to HMAC block size.", "14_Concept_2": "Expand: Iterative HMAC to produce output. T_1=HMAC(PRF, T_0||info||1), T_i=HMAC(PRF, T_{i-1}||info||i). Concatenate T_1||T_2||....", "15_Concept_3": "Modularity: Extract-Expand separated (independent analysis). Can use different salts, info, lengths per expansion.", "16_Concept_4": "TLS 1.3: All keys via HKDF. Handshake secret, app secret, exporter. Clean key separation.", "17_Concept_5": "Adoption: Standard since RFC 5869 (2010). TLS 1.3, QUIC, modern protocols. Simple, proven.", "18_Proofs": "HKDF Extract ≥ PRF under HMAC-PRF assumption. Expand PRF-secure via iteration.", "19_Experiments": "HKDF benchmark. Extract ~1µs, Expand ~1µs per output block. Entropy extraction quality.", "20_Implementation": "RFC 5869 | OpenSSL EVP_PKEY_hkdf | libsodium | Python cryptography | Rust hkdf"},
            {"1_ID": "P025", "2_Title": "Double Ratchet Algorithm", "3_Year": 2016, "4_Authors": "Perrin, T.; Marlinspike, M.", "5_Venue": "Signal Protocol", "6_URL": "https://signal.org/docs/specifications/doubleratchet/", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "Forward secrecy IM via DH ratchet + KDF chain. Per-message key rotation. Recoverable if keys lost (future keys derivable). Signal, WhatsApp, Facebook Messenger standard.", "9_Keywords": "messaging, forward-secrecy, ratchet, DH-ratchet, KDF-chain, key-rotation, Signal-Protocol", "10_Threat_Model": "Passive eavesdropper, temporary key compromise, delayed decryption", "11_Security_Goals": "Forward secrecy (per-message), break-in recovery (future key derivation), message independence", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: DH secure, KDF sound. DOES NOT HANDLE: Compromise of both current + next DH keys", "13_Concept_1": "DH Ratchet: Both parties derive shared DH secret each exchange. Update symmetric keys. Per-exchange rotation.", "14_Concept_2": "KDF Chain: Iterative KDF derives per-message keys from shared secret. Chain advances: k_i = KDF(k_{i-1}, 0).", "15_Concept_3": "Forward Secrecy: Past keys deleted after use. Even if current keys compromised, past ciphertexts remain secret.", "16_Concept_4": "Recover-ability: Future message keys derivable from current state (public KDF). Lost keys recoverable without resync.", "17_Concept_5": "Adoption: Signal, WhatsApp, Facebook Messenger, Telegram (optional). Billions daily users. Industry standard.", "18_Proofs": "Perrin & Marlinspike 2016: Informal security argument. Forward secrecy via key deletion.", "19_Experiments": "Signal, WhatsApp. Per-message latency, key storage, recovery time.", "20_Implementation": "Signal Protocol | https://github.com/signalapp/libsignal (Rust) | WhatsApp proprietary | Facebook Messenger"},
            {"1_ID": "P026", "2_Title": "X3DH: Extended Triple Diffie-Hellman", "3_Year": 2016, "4_Authors": "Perrin, T.; Marlinspike, M.", "5_Venue": "Signal Protocol", "6_URL": "https://signal.org/docs/specifications/x3dh/", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "Asynchronous key agreement (no interaction). Users publish identity/prekeys. Initiator derives shared secret offline. Responder same secret without exchange. Signal, OMEMO (XMPP), Wire.", "9_Keywords": "key-agreement, asynchronous, X3DH, Signal-Protocol, prekeys, identity-keys, one-time-prekeys", "10_Threat_Model": "Passive eavesdropper, temporary prekey compromise, compromised key server", "11_Security_Goals": "Key agreement without interaction, forward secrecy (prekeys), authentication (identity keys)", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: DH secure, prekeys fresh. DOES NOT HANDLE: Compromised server, identity key compromise", "13_Concept_1": "Prekeys: Each user publishes identity key (long-term) + prekeys (ephemeral). Initiator selects prekey, derives secret.", "14_Concept_2": "Four DH Operations: (1) initiator ephemeral - responder prekey, (2) initiator identity - responder prekey, (3) initiator ephemeral - responder identity, (4) initiator identity - responder identity.", "15_Concept_3": "Shared Secret: KDF derives from DH secrets. Different DH ops contribute differently (forward secrecy via ephemeral).", "16_Concept_4": "Asynchronous: Initiator derives secret from responder's public keys (no interaction). Responder derives same from initiator's identity.", "17_Concept_5": "Adoption: Signal, OMEMO (XMPP), Wire. Asynchronous messaging standard. 1-to-1 + group chats.", "18_Proofs": "Informal security. DH hardness + KDF randomness → key agreement security.", "19_Experiments": "Signal, OMEMO. Key agreement latency (offline), message encryption start-time.", "20_Implementation": "Signal Protocol | https://github.com/signalapp/libsignal | OMEMO XEP-0384 | Wire"},
            {"1_ID": "P027", "2_Title": "TweetNaCl: High-Speed Cryptography Library", "3_Year": 2014, "4_Authors": "Bernstein, D.J.; Lange, T.; Schwabe, P.", "5_Venue": "Cryptology ePrint Archive", "6_URL": "https://tweetnacl.cr.yp.to/", "7_DOI": "https://eprint.iacr.org/2014/874", "8_Abstract": "Portable C crypto (Curve25519, ChaCha20, Poly1305, Ed25519). 5000 lines readable. No side-channels, simple verification. Embedded systems, JavaScript, Python.", "9_Keywords": "cryptography-library, minimal, Curve25519, ChaCha20, Poly1305, Ed25519, portable, TweetNaCl", "10_Threat_Model": "Software vulnerabilities, side-channel attacks, complex implementation bugs", "11_Security_Goals": "Correct implementation, no side-channels, auditable code, portable across hardware", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: C compiler correct, underlying crypto secure. DOES NOT HANDLE: Hardware-level attacks", "13_Concept_1": "Minimal Crypto: 5000 lines C (readable). Curve25519, ChaCha20, Poly1305, Ed25519, SHA-512 only.", "14_Concept_2": "Portable: Pure C (no assembly). Constant-time operations. No lookups, branches via secrets.", "15_Concept_3": "Verification: Small size allows auditing. Formal verification research (ProVerif).", "16_Concept_4": "Adoption: libsodium based on TweetNaCl. JavaScript (TweetNaCl.js), Python (PyNaCl).", "17_Concept_5": "Philosophy: Security via simplicity. Contrast huge libraries (OpenSSL 500k+). Education + embedded systems.", "18_Proofs": "Informal. Simplicity reduces bugs. Constant-time prevents timing attacks.", "19_Experiments": "Code review (academic). Benchmark vs OpenSSL (comparable performance). Formal verification research.", "20_Implementation": "https://tweetnacl.cr.yp.to | https://github.com/tweetnacljs/tweetnacl-js | PyNaCl | apt install libsodium"},
            {"1_ID": "P028", "2_Title": "Ed25519: Elliptic Curve Signatures", "3_Year": 2011, "4_Authors": "Bernstein, D.J.; Duif, N.; et al.", "5_Venue": "EUROCRYPT 2011", "6_URL": "https://ed25519.cr.yp.to/", "7_DOI": "https://eprint.iacr.org/2011/368", "8_Abstract": "Fast, safe EdDSA (Edwards curve). Deterministic k (via hash). Constant-time, no branching. ~5ms sign, ~8ms verify ARM. Deployed SSH, TLS 1.3, GPG.", "9_Keywords": "digital-signature, Ed25519, EdDSA, Edwards-curve, constant-time, deterministic, cryptography", "10_Threat_Model": "Signature forgery, timing attacks, nonce reuse vulnerability", "11_Security_Goals": "Unforgeability (EUF-CMA), constant-time, deterministic signing (no nonce reuse)", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Discrete-log hard (~2^128), hash collision-resistant. DOES NOT HANDLE: Quantum Shor", "13_Concept_1": "EdDSA: Deterministic k = HMAC(secret, message). No nonce reuse (k determined by msg). Constant-time.", "14_Concept_2": "Edwards Curve: x^2 + y^2 = 1 + d*x^2*y^2 (mod p). Unified addition (no branching). p = 2^255 - 19.", "15_Concept_3": "Point Compression: 32-byte representation (y + sign bit of x). Compact + efficient.", "16_Concept_4": "Performance: ~5ms sign, ~8ms verify ARM (slower than ECDSA but acceptable). No key recovery parameter.", "17_Concept_5": "Adoption: SSH (OpenSSH), TLS 1.3, GPG, Wireguard, Tor. Preferred new systems (no nonce reuse).", "18_Proofs": "EUF-CMA under DL + random oracle (Fiat-Shamir heuristic). 128-bit security.", "19_Experiments": "ARM Cortex-A72, x86-64. Sign/verify latency, batch verification.", "20_Implementation": "https://ed25519.cr.yp.to | libsodium | OpenSSL 1.1.1+ | OpenSSH | Rust ed25519"},
            {"1_ID": "P029", "2_Title": "AEAD Composition: AES-GCM & ChaCha20-Poly1305", "3_Year": 2018, "4_Authors": "NIST, Bernstein, D.J., Langley, A.", "5_Venue": "Industry Standard", "6_URL": "https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-38d/final", "7_DOI": "10.6028/NIST.SP.800-38D", "8_Abstract": "Authenticated encryption AES-GCM (AES + Galois field multiplication). ChaCha20-Poly1305 alternative. Both NIST-approved. AES-GCM faster with hardware, ChaCha20 software.", "9_Keywords": "AEAD, AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305, authenticated-encryption, Galois-Counter-Mode, composition", "10_Threat_Model": "Passive eavesdropper, active forgery (CCA2), no nonce reuse", "11_Security_Goals": "IND-CPA (encryption) + unforgeability (authentication), nonce-based, no timing leaks", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: AES/ChaCha20 keystream random, GCM/Poly1305 secure. DOES NOT HANDLE: Nonce reuse", "13_Concept_1": "AES-GCM: (1) AES-CTR encrypts plaintext, (2) Poly1305 authenticates ciphertext||AAD, (3) Nonce 96-bit (usually).", "14_Concept_2": "Galois Counter Mode: GCM multiplication in GF(2^128). Auth tag via polynomial evaluation (similar Poly1305).", "15_Concept_3": "ChaCha20-Poly1305: ChaCha20 keystream (faster software), Poly1305 MAC (simpler than GCM).", "16_Concept_4": "Performance: AES-GCM faster with AES-NI (~1-2 cycles/byte). ChaCha20 ~3 cycles/byte (consistent).", "17_Concept_5": "Deployment: AES-GCM TLS 1.3 (hardware), ChaCha20 TLS 1.3 (software/mobile). Both equally secure.", "18_Proofs": "IND-CCA2 under AES/ChaCha20 + GCM/Poly1305 security (composition).", "19_Experiments": "TLS 1.3 performance. OpenSSL benchmarks x86/ARM. Handshake/record encryption speed.", "20_Implementation": "NIST SP 800-38D | OpenSSL AES-GCM | RFC 7539 ChaCha20-Poly1305 | Boringssl, libsodium"},
            {"1_ID": "P030", "2_Title": "Threshold Cryptography: Secret Sharing", "3_Year": 2000, "4_Authors": "Shamir, A. (1979), Desmedt, Y.", "5_Venue": "Handbook of Applied Cryptography", "6_URL": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shamir%27s_Secret_Sharing", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "Split secret into k shares, any t reconstruct (t≤k). Linear algebra GF(p). Applications: key escrow, distributed signing, Byzantine agreement.", "9_Keywords": "threshold-cryptography, secret-sharing, Shamir, linear-algebra, GF(p), key-escrow, distributed", "10_Threat_Model": "Passive share compromise, colluding attackers (< t shares), secret reconstruction attacks", "11_Security_Goals": "Reconstruction (t shares sufficient), Unrecoverable (< t shares learn nothing), perfect secrecy", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Shares uniform random, GF(p) secure. DOES NOT HANDLE: Compromised > t shares", "13_Concept_1": "Polynomial Secret Sharing: Secret = polynomial constant term f(0). Evaluate f(1), f(2), ..., f(k). Any t points reconstruct f.", "14_Concept_2": "Linear Algebra: f(x) = a_0 + a_1*x + ... + a_{t-1}*x^{t-1} (GF(p)). Interpolate via Lagrange (t points).", "15_Concept_3": "Perfect Secrecy: < t shares give no info (adversary has uniform distribution of secrets). Unconditional.", "16_Concept_4": "Verifiable Secret Sharing (VSS): Add commitments to coefficients. Shares verifiable without secret. Prevents cheating.", "17_Concept_5": "Applications: Key escrow (split master key), Distributed signing (threshold ECDSA), Byzantine agreement, MPC.", "18_Proofs": "Shamir 1979: < t shares → uniform secret distribution (information theory). Perfect secrecy.", "19_Experiments": "Shamir sharing reconstruction time. Polynomial evaluation/interpolation speed. VSS verification.", "20_Implementation": "Python secretsharing | libgcrypt (GCM) | MPC frameworks | Ethereum Threshold signatures"},
            {"1_ID": "P031", "2_Title": "Zero-Knowledge Proofs: General Framework", "3_Year": 1985, "4_Authors": "Goldwasser, S.; Micali, S.; Rackoff, C.", "5_Venue": "STOC 1985", "6_URL": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero-knowledge_proof", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "Prove statement true without revealing witness. Completeness (true → accept), Soundness (false → reject), Zero-Knowledge (no info). Applications privacy, authentication.", "9_Keywords": "zero-knowledge, proof, completeness, soundness, zero-knowledge-property, cryptography, privacy", "10_Threat_Model": "Cheating prover, curious verifier, eavesdropper, colluding parties", "11_Security_Goals": "Completeness (honest accept), Soundness (cheating reject), Zero-knowledge (no leakage)", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Computational hardness (DL, factoring). DOES NOT HANDLE: Quantum Shor", "13_Concept_1": "Completeness: If witness exists, honest prover → verifier accept (probability 1).", "14_Concept_2": "Soundness: No witness → cheating prover rejects (probability > 2/3 after ≤ 3 rounds).", "15_Concept_3": "Zero-Knowledge: Verifier learns nothing beyond truth (simulator generates same transcript).", "16_Concept_4": "Interactive vs Non-Interactive: Sigma protocols (3 rounds) vs Fiat-Shamir (hash → non-interactive).", "17_Concept_5": "Applications: Privacy (no secrets leaked), Authentication (identity proof), Blockchain (Zcash, rollups).", "18_Proofs": "Goldwasser et al. 1985: Formal framework. Complete, sound, zero-knowledge (simulator-based).", "19_Experiments": "Sigma protocols (interactive), Fiat-Shamir (non-interactive). Proof size, verification time.", "20_Implementation": "Libsnark, CircomSNARK | Bulletproofs | Tornado.cash | zkSync, Polygon (rollups)"},
            {"1_ID": "P032", "2_Title": "zk-SNARK: Succinct Non-Interactive Argument", "3_Year": 2012, "4_Authors": "Bitansky, N.; Chiesa, A.; et al.", "5_Venue": "EUROCRYPT 2013", "6_URL": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero-knowledge_proof", "7_DOI": "https://eprint.iacr.org/2012/215", "8_Abstract": "Succinct ZK proofs (~200B), non-interactive, fast verification (~10ms). Groth16 pairing-based. Zcash shielded tx, Ethereum rollups. Trusted setup required.", "9_Keywords": "zk-SNARK, succinct, zero-knowledge, non-interactive, pairing-based, Groth16, Ethereum", "10_Threat_Model": "Cheating prover, verifier privacy, circuit satisfiability, trusted setup leak", "11_Security_Goals": "Succinctness (small proof), non-interactive, zero-knowledge, completeness, soundness", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Pairing-friendly groups secure, trusted setup honest, hash random oracle. DOES NOT HANDLE: Setup leak", "13_Concept_1": "Groth16 (2016): Pairing-based most efficient. CRS (common reference string) generated trusted. Proof ~200B.", "14_Concept_2": "Constraint System: Boolean circuit → arithmetic circuit (GF(p)). QAP (Quadratic Arithmetic Program) satisfiability.", "15_Concept_3": "Trusted Setup: Ceremony generates CRS from random tape (destroyed). Participants must be honest (any 1 OK).", "16_Concept_4": "Verification: Fast ~10ms (pairing check), independent circuit size. Proof publicly verifiable.", "17_Concept_5": "Applications: Zcash (shielded tx), Ethereum rollups (validity proofs), privacy, scalability. Research: transparent setup.", "18_Proofs": "Groth16 (EUROCRYPT 2016): Knowledge of exponent (KoE) assumption. Zero-knowledge (simulator-based).", "19_Experiments": "Circuit size (gates) vs proof time. Groth16 fastest. Proof size ~200B independent circuit.", "20_Implementation": "Libsnark (C++) | CircomSNARK | Zcash | zkSync | Polygon Hermez | Ethereum 2.0 rollups"},
            {"1_ID": "P033", "2_Title": "Bulletproofs: Range Proofs & Aggregation", "3_Year": 2018, "4_Authors": "Bunz, B.; Bootle, J.; et al.", "5_Venue": "IEEE S&P 2018", "6_URL": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulletproofs", "7_DOI": "https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/1066", "8_Abstract": "Logarithmic range proofs (~600B per output, ~70ms verify). Aggregatable (multiple proofs). No trusted setup. Monero (amount hiding), Ethereum scaling.", "9_Keywords": "zero-knowledge, range-proof, Bulletproofs, logarithmic, aggregation, no-trusted-setup, privacy", "10_Threat_Model": "Cheating prover (invalid range), multiple proofs coordination, computational hardness", "11_Security_Goals": "Range proofs, aggregation efficiency, zero-knowledge, no setup (transparent)", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Discrete-log hard (DLP). DOES NOT HANDLE: Quantum Shor", "13_Concept_1": "Inner Product Argument: Core technique. Proves inner product zero via iterative reduction (log rounds).", "14_Concept_2": "Range Proof: Prove a ∈ [0, 2^n]. Represent a binary, prove each bit via Bulletproofs. ~log(n) size.", "15_Concept_3": "Aggregation: Multiple range proofs combined. Single verification ~log(n+m) (m = # proofs). Scalability.", "16_Concept_4": "No Trusted Setup: Transparent (no CRS needed). Only DLP assumption (DL secure curves).", "17_Concept_5": "Deployment: Monero (May 2019, ~650B per tx). Ethereum Rollups (scaling). RingCT variant.", "18_Proofs": "Bunz et al. 2018: Soundness under discrete-log. Zero-knowledge (simulator). Logarithmic size proof.", "19_Experiments": "Proof generation/verification time vs range. Aggregation benefits with multiple proofs.", "20_Implementation": "https://github.com/dalek-cryptography/bulletproofs | Monero | Ethereum Rollups | libdalek (Rust)"},
            {"1_ID": "P034", "2_Title": "Schnorr Signature Scheme", "3_Year": 1989, "4_Authors": "Schnorr, C.P.", "5_Venue": "EUROCRYPT 1989", "6_URL": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schnorr_signature", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "Efficient signature (nonce-based like ECDSA). Batch verification + aggregation (multisig). Larger signature (ECDSA smaller), simpler analysis. Bitcoin Taproot (2021), Ethereum possible.", "9_Keywords": "digital-signature, Schnorr, batch-verification, aggregation, multi-signature, Bitcoin-Taproot", "10_Threat_Model": "Signature forgery, nonce reuse, multi-party aggregation attacks", "11_Security_Goals": "Unforgeability (EUF-CMA), batch verification, signature aggregation", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: DLP hard, hash collision-resistant. DOES NOT HANDLE: Quantum Shor, nonce reuse", "13_Concept_1": "Signing: k random, R = k*G, s = k + H(R||m)*d (mod n). Signature (R, s) ~64B. Simple math.", "14_Concept_2": "Verification: Check s*G = R + H(R||m)*Q. Linear algebra (vs ECDSA pairing complexity).", "15_Concept_3": "Batch Verification: Multiple sigs verified as single check (amortized cost). Linear time savings.", "16_Concept_4": "Signature Aggregation: Multiple signers → single signature (multisig). Threshold Schnorr via MPC.", "17_Concept_5": "Adoption: Bitcoin Taproot (2021, optional). Ethereum possible. Simpler than ECDSA multisig.", "18_Proofs": "Schnorr 1989: Unforgeability under DLP + random oracle. Batch verification correctness.", "19_Experiments": "Bitcoin Taproot analysis. Batch vs individual verification. Signature aggregation time.", "20_Implementation": "Bitcoin Taproot | Ethereum 2.0 (research) | libsecp256k1 | Rust schnorrkel | BLS signatures"},
            {"1_ID": "P035", "2_Title": "Multi-Party Computation (MPC)", "3_Year": 2000, "4_Authors": "Yao, A. (1982), Goldreich, O.; Micali, S.; Wigderson, A.", "5_Venue": "Handbook of Cryptography", "6_URL": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secure_multi-party_computation", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "Compute function on private inputs securely. None learn secrets. Garbled circuits, secret sharing. Applications: voting, auction, machine learning.", "9_Keywords": "MPC, secure-computation, garbled-circuits, secret-sharing, multi-party, privacy-preserving", "10_Threat_Model": "Semi-honest/malicious participants, colluding parties, information leakage", "11_Security_Goals": "Privacy (secrets hidden), correctness (result accurate), independence of inputs", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Symmetric crypto secure, secret sharing sound. DOES NOT HANDLE: Active attacks (need zero-knowledge)", "13_Concept_1": "Yao Garbled Circuits: Encoding circuits gates, evaluator learns only function output. Two-party MPC.", "14_Concept_2": "Secret Sharing: Distribute input shares. Compute on shares (additively homomorphic). Reconstruct result.", "15_Concept_3": "Oblivious Transfer (OT): Transfer 1 of 2 messages (sender doesn't know which). Core building block MPC.", "16_Concept_4": "Malicious Adversaries: Add zero-knowledge proofs (correctness). Higher overhead than semi-honest.", "17_Concept_5": "Applications: Distributed voting (privacy), sealed auctions (fairness), machine learning (data privacy). Deployment research.", "18_Proofs": "Yao (1982): Garbled circuits correct/secure. GMW (1987): Information-theoretic MPC (secret sharing).", "19_Experiments": "MPC evaluation circuits vs centralized. OT, garbling, reconstruction time. Communication overhead.", "20_Implementation": "SCALE-MAMBA | MP-SPDZ | Threshold ECDSA (signing) | Private machine learning (PPML)"},
            {"1_ID": "P036", "2_Title": "Homomorphic Encryption: Encrypted Computation", "3_Year": 2009, "4_Authors": "Gentry, C.", "5_Venue": "STOC 2009", "6_URL": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homomorphic_encryption", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "Compute on ciphertexts without decryption. Fully homomorphic encryption (FHE): arbitrary circuits. Gentry 2009 breakthrough. Practical (slow) via optimizations.", "9_Keywords": "homomorphic-encryption, FHE, encrypted-computation, privacy, cloud-computing, TFHE", "10_Threat_Model": "Untrusted server (cloud), data privacy, function evaluation without plaintext", "11_Security_Goals": "Semantic security (IND-CPA), arbitrary function evaluation, ciphertext domination (no interaction)", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Lattice problems hard (LWE, Ring-LWE). DOES NOT HANDLE: Performance (slow 1000-1M×)", "13_Concept_1": "Additive HE: E(m1) + E(m2) = E(m1 + m2). Supported by partially HE (Paillier, ElGamal).", "14_Concept_2": "Multiplicative HE: E(m1) * E(m2) = E(m1 * m2). Rare (RSA textbook, Rabin).", "15_Concept_3": "Fully HE: Both + and *. Gentry construction: SWHE + bootstrapping (refresh ciphertext). Circular security.", "16_Concept_4": "Noise: Ciphertext noise increases per operation. Bootstrapping decrypts/re-encrypts → noise reset. Overhead.", "17_Concept_5": "Practical FHE: TFHE (2016) faster bootstrapping. Private machine learning (neural networks on encrypted data).", "18_Proofs": "Gentry 2009: FHE from SWHE + bootstrapping. Semantic security under LWE assumption.", "19_Experiments": "Evaluation time vs circuit depth. Bootstrapping cost. Private ML on encrypted data (slow).", "20_Implementation": "HElib | SEAL | TFHE | Private machine learning frameworks | Encrypted statistical analysis"},
            {"1_ID": "P037", "2_Title": "Differential Privacy: Statistical Privacy", "3_Year": 2006, "4_Authors": "Dwork, C.", "5_Venue": "ICALP 2006", "6_URL": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differential_privacy", "7_DOI": "None", "8_Abstract": "Formal privacy guarantee for statistical queries. Adding noise (Laplace/Gaussian) → indistinguishable adjacent databases. Apple, Google, Uber deployment.", "9_Keywords": "differential-privacy, privacy-preserving, statistical-database, Laplace-mechanism, privacy-budget", "10_Threat_Model": "Database queries reveal individual records, linkage attacks, inference attacks", "11_Security_Goals": "Individual privacy guarantee, query answer accuracy (utility), composition bounds", "12_Assumptions_Limitations": "ASSUMES: Noise mechanism correct, privacy budget tuned. DOES NOT HANDLE: Side-channel (non-query attacks)", "13_Concept_1": "ε-Differential Privacy: P(A(D) ∈ S) ≤ e^ε * P(A(D') ∈ S) (adjacent databases D, D'). Noise calibrated to query sensitivity.", "14_Concept_2": "Laplace Mechanism: Add Laplace(0, Δf/ε) noise to query result. Δf = max difference removing one record.", "15_Concept_3": "Privacy Budget: ε allows composition bounds. Smaller ε stricter privacy. ε=0.1 (strict), ε=1 (loose).", "16_Concept_4": "Composition: Multiple queries accumulate privacy loss. Strong composition: ε' ≈ sqrt(k)*ε (k queries).", "17_Concept_5": "Deployment: Apple (on-device learning), Google (Census aggregation), Uber (movement patterns). Practical privacy-utility.", "18_Proofs": "Dwork 2006: ε-differential privacy definition. Laplace mechanism achieves DP. Composition theorems.", "19_Experiments": "Privacy-utility tradeoff. Query accuracy vs privacy loss (ε). Real datasets (Census, NYC taxi).", "20_Implementation": "OpenDP (Python) | Google DP library | Apple differential privacy | IBM diffprivlib | TensorFlow Privacy"},
